  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1239 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nThe tilted low level ridging pattern that holds onto the upper TX \ncoast continues to funnel intermittent moist and dry pockets of low \nlevel air into South Central TX and this has us not feeling too \nconfident on the rain chances for Wednesday. With out SE counties \nprojected to see 1.5-1.7 inch PWat values, it would seem that low \nlevel convection would be somewhat isolated. However, that SW flow \noverhead will be capable of ejecting some weak vorticity out of the \nhigher terrain as the SW shear over the eastern Pacific should have \na better connection to weakening Tropical Storm Vance. The current \nmodel cycle indicates most runs holding back any rain chance \nincreases for Wednesday, but there has been a large sway towards a \nwetter environment developing Wednesday night into Thursday morning \nover western counties and spreading slowly northeast across much of \nthe area through late Friday. The 00Z solutions looked at thus far \ndon't seem to show good agreement on what the surface conditions \nwill look like during this period, and the GFS could be an outlier \nin generating a shortwave strong enough to bring its own frontal \nsystem into TX Friday. The model blends are therefore favored, and \nthe more likely scenario is continued low level dry air entrainment \ninto eastern counties to limit the rainfall potential to mainly \nalong and west of I-35 when the shortwave pushes through. Any \nchanges to Vance could also disrupt this weather trend, so the \nfeeling here is that our PoPs for this Thursday into Friday should \nbe treated with a degree of skepticism. Should Vance's trend impact \nour area in a more \"positive\" way, it will be nice to have later \nshifts inherit the current messaging that a heavy rain event of 1-2 \ninch rains and potentially higher is well within the realm of \npossibility for Thursday and Friday. \n\nTemperatures remain seasonably mild warm and should be fairly steady \nthrough mid week. Assuming the enhanced rain chances are going to \npan out for Thursday and Friday, some relatively lower high \ntemperatures are already getting baked into the guidance blends for \nthat period.",
